add('# Hoops Dynasty — Score-state and late-game update')
add('21 October 2026. Baseline: published Phase 6 / unchanged Phase 5 engine, commit 87c30bf1d91881cb5eae09e0f0447089137bb3a4. This is a targeted coaching-policy implementation and paired evaluation, not a new broad engine rewrite.')
add('\n## Implemented behavior')
add('1. Lead protection: inside the final two minutes, non-transition offense with a lead spends more setup time, reserving at least nine shot-clock seconds for the existing passing/shot sequence. Setup is capped at 15 seconds. Fast-break opportunities remain available. If the remaining game clock is shorter than the shot clock, the existing hold/foul behavior remains. No field-goal or free-throw probability changes.')
add('2. Trailing offense: urgency extends to a 12-point deficit in the last minute. A team down exactly three seeks a three with 15 seconds or less, rather than 12; multi-possession deficits use three-point urgency with six seconds or less. Short-clock attempts respect the actual shot clock instead of always spending up to three seconds. Ordinary contest, turnover, foul and accuracy resolvers remain in use.')
add('3. Rotation response: the old policy immediately excluded starters on BOTH teams at a 20-point margin from the beginning of Q4. The revised settled-game thresholds are 30 points with six minutes left, 24 with four, and 18 with two. A team leading by 20 with eight minutes left may prioritize reserves for workload protection. A team down 8–26 during the final quarter of regulation time can prioritize better available players while they have at least 75 energy, remain below the existing stint limit, and have not exceeded their target minutes by three. Normal injury, ejection, foul and workload eligibility still applies.')
add('4. Substitution explanations identify “chasing the game” and “protecting workload with a large lead” in the existing game logs. These policies reverse when the score context changes. They do not alter stored player ratings, force a winner, cap scores, or apply a trailing-team accuracy boost.')
add('These thresholds are explicit simulation assumptions. They are not presented as measured NBA/WNBA coaching frequencies. The same clock-based policies operate within each league’s existing game length, shot clock and foul rules. Historical format compatibility is tested separately from historical statistical calibration.')
add('\n## What the requested targets actually measure')
add('Quarter stacking = Var(Q1+Q2+Q3+Q4 margins) − sum Var(individual quarter margins) = twice the sum of the six cross-quarter covariances. Units are points squared. Overtime is excluded from this statistic. Positive values indicate reinforcement across quarters; negative values indicate partial offset. This identity is descriptive, not causal.')
add('The retained full NBA baseline has +42.98 points²; the available official NBA sample has −44.18. However, the original 160-game identical-roster NBA fixture was already −25.70. Positive league stacking cannot therefore be attributed solely to the mirror fixture’s randomness. Different team-quality mixtures matter. Mirror games also retain stochastic possessions, fatigue, fouls and rotations; they do not isolate shooting luck alone.')
