add('\n## Interpretation and remaining limits')
add('Observed outcome: NBA mean margin fell 14.79 → 13.84, 20+ margins fell 28.70% → 24.39%, and quarter stacking fell +43.71 → +16.71. NBA mirror mean margin fell 12.94 → 11.97 and stacking moved −23.15 → −49.49. The full-league negative-stacking target remains unmet. WNBA mean margin fell 12.81 → 12.31 and within-ten games rose 49.79% → 53.96%, but 30+ margins increased 5.83% → 6.67%, regulation-margin variance was essentially unchanged (232.47 → 233.08), and mirror stacking did not improve across all 400 trials. WNBA evidence is mixed, not a solved variance problem. No further tuning was justified solely to hit one covariance number.')
add('The changes address observable strategy weaknesses. They are not a calibrated universal solution for blowouts. The mirror and full-league results must be assessed separately: a mirror improvement does not prove the same effect across differing rosters. The first three quarters of a reset matchup are intentionally untouched by these late-game policies; early efficiency separation remains an unresolved issue. The two components were tested together, so their individual causal contributions are not estimated.')
add('The numerical quarter-stacking target was not enforced with score adjustments or a shared random multiplier. Wide real-sample uncertainty, roster-mixture differences and only two NBA/four WNBA season seeds limit conclusions. No claim is made that every league or historical era is now statistically balanced.')
